      "chapterTitle": "I. 23:45 — The Blue Plastic Bag",
      "heading": "I. 23:45 — The Blue Plastic Bag",
      "body": "The siren had been turned off two hundred yards before the hospital gates, but the pulsing red light of the revolving beacon continued to sweep across the wet asphalt of Dhakuria like a rhythmic, bloody blade.\n\nIt was eleven-forty-five on a Tuesday night in early December. The winter fog of south Kolkata had settled over the stagnant water of the lakes, smelling of diesel exhaust, burning leaves, and the cold damp of the Hooghly basin.\n\nDr. Rahul Sen stood on the concrete emergency apron, holding the frame of the wheeled stretcher with both hands as four hospital orderlies in sweat-stained maroon uniforms dragged it down the metal ramp of the ambulance.\n\nOn the rubber mattress lay his father, Debabrata Sen.\n\nSeventy-six years old. Retired Postal Accounts Superintendent. A man who had walked five miles every morning around the Rabindra Sarobar lake since the winter of 1974, who had never spent a single night in a hospital bed in his life, and who had never allowed another human being to lace his shoes.\n\nNow, Debabrata's head was thrown back against the vinyl headrest, his grey hair matted with vomit, his dentures sitting in a paper cup taped to the side rail. His breathing was not human breathing. It was a heavy, wet, mechanical snore—a sound known in clinical textbooks as stertor, but which sounded to Rahul like dirty river water boiling violently inside an iron kettle.\n\nThe triage physician, a boy of twenty-seven with bloodshot eyes and an unbuttoned white apron over a blue t-shirt, pulled back Debabrata's left eyelid with his thumb.\n\nHe shone a pencil torch into the pupil.\n\n'Non-reactive,' the doctor muttered to the nurse. 'Six millimeters. Fixed.'\n\nHe turned the beam to the right eye.\n\n'Sluggish. Three millimeters. GCS is four. Motor response: decerebrate posturing on the left side.'\n\nHe looked at Rahul.\n\n'You are the son?'\n\n'Yes,' Rahul said.\n\nHis voice was steady. He was forty-four years old, an Associate Professor of Quantitative Economics at Jadavpur University, a man whose entire intellectual life had been dedicated to the mathematical modeling of stochastic systems and risk variables. He knew what a Glasgow Coma Scale of four meant. He knew what a fixed and dilated left pupil meant in a seventy-six-year-old hypertensive patient who had collapsed in his bathroom forty minutes after dinner.\n\nIt meant that a major cerebral artery—most likely the lenticulostriate branch of the middle cerebral artery—had ruptured under two hundred and twenty millimeters of mercury pressure, pouring forty or fifty milliliters of arterial blood directly into the left basal ganglia, ripping through the internal capsule, compressing the brainstem into the narrow stone aperture of the foramen magnum.\n\nIt meant that his father was already three-quarters dead.\n\n'We need to intubate immediately,' the triage doctor said, walking alongside the trolley as it rattled through the double automatic doors into the emergency bay. 'Blood pressure is two hundred and forty over one hundred and thirty. We are putting him on IV labetalol and mannitol. The CT scan is being prepped in basement two.'\n\nA triage nurse with silver bangles clinking against her plastic wristband caught Rahul by the elbow before he could follow the stretcher past the red barrier line.\n\n'Sir, you cannot go inside the trauma bay,' she said. Her voice was neither unkind nor sympathetic; it was the flat, waterproof voice of an emergency worker who processed seventy human catastrophes before midnight. 'Please complete the admission paperwork at Counter 4. And take this.'\n\nShe handed him a semi-transparent blue plastic grocery bag with *Reliance Fresh* printed in green block letters across its belly.\n\nThe bag was warm from the ambulance heater.\n\nRahul took it.\n\nHe looked down into the plastic opening.\n\nInside the bag were the clothes his father had been wearing ninety minutes ago when he had stood at the dining table in their Jadavpur flat:\n\nA pair of faded brown rubber bathroom slippers, size eight, with the right heel worn down flat from his father's asymmetrical gait;\nA black nylon folding umbrella tied with a strip of yellow knitting yarn because the snap fastener had torn away during the monsoon of 2018;\nA scarred brown leather spectacles case wrapped with two rounds of transparent packaging tape around the hinge;\nA steel key ring holding four keys: the brass lever key for the flat's front door, the small nickel key for the Godrej steel almirah, the long key for the postal pension locker at the GPO, and a rusted padlock key to the terrace clothesline;\nAnd a clear plastic pouch containing eighty-four rupees in coins and two crumpled twenty-rupee notes that his father had received as change from the fishmonger at the Lake Market that morning.\n\nSeventy-six years of human discipline, fifty-two years of immaculate government service, thirty-eight years of marriage to late Anjali Sen, two children raised, educated, and launched into the world.\n\nAll of it had been condensed into one point eight kilograms of damp plastic resting in Rahul's hand under the fluorescent tube of an emergency corridor.",
