      "id": "sec-hc-3",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II. 00:30 — The Geometry of the Fifth Floor",
      "heading": "II. 00:30 — The Geometry of the Fifth Floor",
      "body": "By twelve-thirty, the emergency bay had handed Debabrata over to the machines, and the machines had carried him to the fifth floor.\n\nThe fifth floor was the Neuro-Intensive Care Unit.\n\nIt was reached by a heavy, shuddering service elevator that took forty-two seconds to climb five storeys, its stainless steel walls scratched with old graffiti and dented by thousands of gurney collisions. When the doors opened, the atmosphere changed instantly.\n\nDownstairs in emergency, there had been noise, speed, shouting, blood on the linoleum, the smell of wound spirit and vomit.\n\nUp here, there was only cold, pressurized air and the terrifying, low-frequency hum of three dozen cooling fans.\n\nThe corridor was fifty yards long, paved with beige non-slip vinyl tiles that had been polished until they reflected the square fluorescent ceiling light panels with watery, swimming distortion. Along the left wall was a continuous handrail of brushed stainless steel; along the right wall were twelve low vinyl benches bolted to the floor with zinc-plated expansion bolts.\n\nAt the far end of the corridor were two heavy double doors of frosted acoustic glass with a brass sign screwed into the lintel: *NEURO-ICU — RESTRICTED ENTRY — AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY.*\n\nAbove the door, a digital wall clock displayed the time in sharp, glowing red LED numerals:\n\n*00:34:12*\n\nRahul sat down on the bench nearest to the double doors.\n\nHe placed the blue Reliance Fresh bag between his feet on the floor, tucking it against his shins so that no passing orderly with a laundry cart would kick it into the corner.\n\nHis phone vibrated in his jacket pocket.\n\nHe pulled it out.\n\nThe display showed three missed calls from his wife Piyali, two WhatsApp messages from his department head at Jadavpur University, and a battery indicator that had dropped to fourteen percent, pulsing with a pale amber outline.\n\nHe typed a quick text message to Piyali:\n\n*CT scan done. Massive bleed in left basal ganglia. Intubated on ventilator. ICU Bed 7. Don't wake Soumik. Stay home. Will call when doctor comes out.*\n\nHe pressed send.\n\nBefore he could lock the screen, the billing clerk from the third-floor administrative desk appeared at the corner of the corridor holding a yellow clipboard.\n\nHe was a thin young man with an ID card hanging from a blue lanyard: *Subhasish Das — Patient Financial Services.*\n\n'Dr. Rahul Sen?' the clerk asked, adjusting his ballpoint pen.\n\n'Yes,' Rahul said, rising halfway from the bench.\n\n'Sir, the initial admission deposit for Neuro-ICU is forty-five thousand rupees,' the clerk said, his tone polite, rehearsed, entirely devoid of malice. 'This covers the first twenty-four hours of bed charges, monitor usage, and basic nursing. But the doctor has ordered three vials of intravenous Edaravone and continuous infusions of Mannitol and Noradrenaline. Those medications must be procured from the third-floor central pharmacy immediately.'\n\nHe handed Rahul a carbonized pink requisition slip.\n\n'Total pharmacy requisition is thirty-eight thousand two hundred rupees, sir. If you pay at Counter 2 by card or UPI, the medicines will be delivered directly to Sister in Bed 7.'\n\nRahul took the slip.\n\nHe looked at the printed columns: *Requisition No. 88412. Drug Code: NEURO-04. Qty: 3. Rate: 11,400.00.*\n\nThirty-eight thousand two hundred rupees for three glass ampoules of clear liquid that would not cure his father's stroke, would not dissolve the forty milliliters of clotted blood compressing his brain tissue, but would merely slow the secondary swelling by four or five percent to buy twelve hours of clinical time.\n\n'Does the hospital accept the West Bengal Health Scheme card?' Rahul asked.\n\nThe clerk gave the small, sympathetic wince of a private hospital administrator delivering bad news to a government employee.\n\n'Sir, this is a private super-specialty facility. We are empanelled for emergency stabilization under the state scheme, but the bed category in Neuro-ICU requires prior authorization from the Medical Superintendent's office, which opens at ten in the morning. For tonight, all pharmacy and consumables must be settled on cash or credit card basis. You can apply for post-facto reimbursement with the discharge summary.'\n\n*Post-facto reimbursement.*\n\nRahul knew that phrase. It was the bureaucratic mantra of the Indian middle class: pay with your savings today, stand in seven government queues tomorrow, submit four signed affidavits in triplicate, and receive thirty-two percent of the claim eighteen months later after the audit committee has disallowed thirty-six separate line items.\n\n'I understand,' Rahul said.\n\nHe took out his wallet, pulled out his State Bank of India debit card, and handed it to the clerk.\n\n'Swipe it at the counter. Send the medicines to Bed 7.'\n\nThe clerk took the card, nodded with quiet professional respect, and hurried down the corridor toward the elevator.\n\nRahul sat back down on the vinyl bench.\n\nThe building hummed around him.\n\nSomewhere beyond the frosted glass doors, inside Bed 7, a motorized ventilator was forcing compressed oxygen into his father's lungs through a plastic endotracheal tube with a soft, rhythmic hiss:\n\n*Three seconds in. Two seconds out.*\n*Three seconds in. Two seconds out.*\n\nSeventy-six years of breathing on his own—through sixty-four monsoons in Calcutta, through the famine years of his childhood, through the student strikes of 1971, through forty years of climbing the stairs to the second-floor flat in Jadavpur carrying ten kilograms of groceries—had ended at eleven o'clock tonight.\n\nFrom this hour forward, Debabrata Sen did not breathe.\n\nA German pneumatic piston breathed for him.",
