      "chapterTitle": "III. 01:12 — The Mathematics of Mortality",
      "heading": "III. 01:12 — The Mathematics of Mortality",
      "body": "By one-twelve in the morning, Rahul was the only person sitting in the southern wing of the fifth floor.\n\nThe cleaning staff had completed their midnight sweep with phenyl-scented mops, leaving the floor damp and cold. A junior security guard in a grey safari suit sat eighty feet away beside the fire exit, his chin resting against his chest, snoring with a soft, fluttering whistle through his nostrils.\n\nRahul looked at his watch.\n\nIt was an HMT Janata mechanical wristwatch with a stainless steel case and a black leather strap.\n\nHis father had given it to him on his eighteenth birthday in August 1998, on the morning he had entered Presidency College to study economics. His father had bought it from a shop on Radha Bazar Street for four hundred and twenty rupees, having saved thirty rupees a month for fourteen months from his postal salary.\n\n*“A mechanical watch does not tell you what the world wants you to do, Rahul,”* his father had told him that morning, winding the small knurled crown with his thumb. *“It tells you what the balance of time is. A battery watch will stop suddenly without warning when the chemical runs dry. But a spring will tell you when it is losing tension. If you wind it every morning with sixteen turns, it will never lie to you.”*\n\nRahul looked at the second hand sweeping across the silver dial.\n\nFor twenty-six years, Rahul had wound that watch every morning at seven o'clock. Sixteen turns of the crown. It had never lost more than four seconds a day.\n\nHe touched his thumb to the crystal.\n\nAs an economist, Rahul had made his reputation by publishing papers on the econometric modeling of household risk. He had published in the *Journal of Development Economics* and the *Indian Economic Review*. He could calculate the Gini coefficient of thirty-two districts in five minutes; he could construct a three-stage least-squares regression to predict agricultural credit default with ninety-four percent accuracy.\n\nHe had always believed—with the serene, intellectual vanity of the academic—that catastrophe could be understood if one possessed sufficient data.\n\nIf a system failed, it failed because of unpriced externalities, because of informational asymmetry, because the decision-makers had miscalculated the variance.\n\nNow, sitting on a vinyl bench outside Bed 7, he realized that all of his mathematics was a decorative child's game designed to distract the human mind from the absolute, ferocious stupidity of death.\n\nThere was no variance here. There was no equilibrium curve.\n\nThere was only a five-millimeter vessel in the brain that had grown brittle over seventy-six years of eating salt, worrying about pension revisions, and walking through the smog of south Kolkata, until the wall of the artery had thinned to the thickness of an onion peel.\n\nAnd then, while his father was reaching for a steel tumbler of water on the dining table, the wall had given way.\n\n*Forty milliliters of blood.*\n\nTwo tablespoons.\n\nA quantity of liquid that would not fill half a tea cup.\n\nThat was all it took to extinguish fifty-two years of memory: the knowledge of every postal circle from Cooch Behar to Midnapore; the memory of his wedding night in 1978 when it had rained so hard that the shamiana had collapsed into the fish curry; the taste of the first winter oranges brought from Assam; the sound of his mother's voice calling him from the kitchen forty years ago.\n\nTwo tablespoons of blood turned a man who could recite the poetry of Jibanananda Das by heart into an eighty-kilogram meat parcel connected to an oxygen hose.\n\nRahul leaned his head back against the painted wall.\n\nThe plaster was cold against his skull.\n\nHe remembered the monsoon of 1988.\n\nHe had been eight years old. It was July, and the southwest monsoon had broken over Calcutta with biblical ferocity. Gariahat was flooded to the waist. The trams had stopped running; the buses were stranded at the Ballygunge depot with water filling their exhaust pipes.\n\nSchool had closed early at one o'clock.\n\nRahul had stood under the dripping concrete awning of South Point High School, his canvas satchel soaked, his leather shoes ruined, crying because the thunder was shaking the window glass.\n\nAnd then, through the brown, swirling floodwaters of Gariahat, his father had appeared.\n\nHe had been riding his black Hercules cycle—the heavy, twenty-two-inch steel roadster that weighed twenty kilograms. His father had rolled his white dhoti up to his thighs, his bare legs covered in black city mud, holding a huge black cotton umbrella with his left hand while steering the heavy iron handlebar with his right.\n\nWhen his father had reached the school gate, he had lifted Rahul up and seated him on the steel crossbar between his arms.\n\nHe had handed Rahul the handle of the umbrella: *“Hold it straight over your head, khoka. Don't let the water hit your chest, or you will get a fever.”*\n\nFor four miles, through water that reached the wheel hubs, through the rain that hammered against the cotton fabric like gravel, his father had pedaled that bicycle through the flooded streets of south Calcutta.\n\nHis father had been completely unprotected. The rain had beaten against his back, soaked his cotton shirt until it was translucent, plastered his hair to his forehead, run in grey streams down his neck.\n\nEvery time the cycle had hit a submerged pothole, his father's arms had tightened around Rahul like two iron bars, keeping him balanced, keeping him high above the filthy water.\n\nWhen they had finally reached the dry courtyard of their house in Jadavpur, his father had lifted Rahul down, examined him from head to toe to make sure not a single drop of floodwater had touched his uniform, and smiled:\n\n*“See? Not even your socks are wet.”*\n\nThen his father had walked to the porch, sat on the stone steps, and coughed for ten minutes, his chest rattling with the cold that would keep him in bed with pleurisy for three weeks.\n\nRahul sat on the vinyl bench on the fifth floor.\n\nHe looked down at his own hands.\n\nHe was forty-four years old. He had a doctorate. He had tenure. He had a bank account with forty-two lakh rupees of mutual funds.\n\nAnd there was no bicycle. There was no umbrella. There was no way to ride between his father and the flood that was drowning him inside Bed 7.",
