      "chapterTitle": "IV. 01:55 — The Sister on the Midnight Red-Eye",
      "heading": "IV. 01:55 — The Sister on the Midnight Red-Eye",
      "body": "At one-fifty-five, his phone buzzed again.\n\n*Shampa Calling.*\n\nRahul answered on the second ring, pressing the phone to his ear with his shoulder while rubbing the back of his neck.\n\n'Dada?' Shampa's voice came through the line, breathless, accompanied by the background roar of jet engines reversing their thrust on wet concrete. 'We just touched down at Dum Dum. The pilot said the temperature outside is fourteen degrees. I am in the fourth row, waiting for the doors to open. How is Baba?'\n\n'He is in the Neuro-ICU, Shampa,' Rahul said. He kept his tone deliberate, measured, low. 'Bed 7. He is stable on the ventilator. The bleeding has stopped expanding according to the repeat scan.'\n\n'Who is the senior doctor?' Shampa's voice rose instantly into the rapid, interrogative rhythm of a corporate human resources executive managing an operational crisis. 'Have you spoken with Dr. Sukanta Roy? The Chief of Neurology? My managing director in Gurgaon gave me his private mobile number! He said Dr. Roy is the top neurosurgeon in Eastern India!'\n\n'Shampa, it is two in the morning,' Rahul said gently. 'The resident on duty is Dr. Ghosh. He is very competent. Dr. Roy will review the case at eight during morning rounds.'\n\n'Why didn't you admit him to Apollo Gleneagles on the EM Bypass?' she demanded, her voice cracking with the frantic, defensive aggression that had characterized her since their mother's death. 'I told you six months ago, Dada, that if Baba ever had an emergency, we should take him to Apollo! They have an advanced stroke unit with digital biplane angiography! Why did you bring him to this old place in Dhakuria?'\n\nRahul took a deep, silent breath through his nose.\n\nHe did not raise his voice.\n\nHe knew what was happening inside his sister's chest.\n\nShampa lived in a gated condominium in Sector 56, Gurgaon, with Italian marble bathrooms, a concierge service, and a company-leased BMW. She earned sixty-five lakh rupees a year. She was brilliant, dynamic, respected across the corporate tech sector for her ability to negotiate complex labor disputes.\n\nAnd she had visited Kolkata exactly twice in the last fourteen months.\n\nOnce for forty-eight hours during Durga Puja, during which she had spent thirty hours attending parties with her school friends from Modern High School, and once for a Sunday lunch in March on her way back from a corporate conference in Dhaka.\n\nWhenever Debabrata had called her on Sunday evenings, she had spoken to him on Bluetooth from her car while driving to the supermarket: *“Yes, Baba, I'm fine, work is crazy, we're launching the Bangalore office next week, make sure you take your blood pressure pills, love you, bye!”*\n\nHer anger now was not about Apollo Gleneagles.\n\nHer anger was the frantic, desperate panic of a child who had realized that she had traded her father's final years for an executive stock option schedule, and who now wanted to use corporate healthcare, private air ambulances, and famous surgeons to rewrite the past thirty-six months of neglect.\n\n'Shampa,' Rahul said softly. 'When the stroke happened at nine-thirty, Baba's airway was obstructed. He was aspirating his saliva. Dhakuria was seven minutes from the house. Apollo was forty-five minutes in the traffic on Prince Anwar Shah Road. If I had tried to reach Apollo, he would have suffered irreversible hypoxic cardiac arrest before we reached Jadavpur police station. Dhakuria saved his breathing.'\n\nA long pause followed from the terminal tarmac.\n\nThrough the phone, Rahul could hear the chimes of the seatbelt signs being turned off, the clatter of overhead luggage bins opening, the mechanical voice of the flight attendant thanking passengers for flying IndiGo.\n\n'I have only my cabin trolley,' Shampa whispered. Her voice had lost its corporate edge, dropping into a small, broken whimper. 'I didn't check any bags. I will take a prepaid taxi from the gate. I will be there in thirty minutes, Dada.'\n\n'Don't take prepaid,' Rahul said. 'The queue at Dum Dum is terrible at two in the morning. Go to the app cab zone on the top deck. Book an Uber Premier. Tell the driver to take the Belgharia Expressway to the Bypass.'\n\n'Okay,' she said.\n\n'Shampa?'\n\n'Yes, Dada?'\n\n'He won't recognize you,' Rahul said quietly. 'His eyes are closed. He is in a deep coma. Prepare yourself before you walk in.'\n\nShe did not answer. The line went dead with a soft electronic click.",
