      "id": "sec-hc-9",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "V. 02:24 — The Roller Suitcase in the Hallway",
      "heading": "V. 02:24 — The Roller Suitcase in the Hallway",
      "body": "The sound arrived before she did: the sharp, rhythmic *clack-clack-clack* of four polyurethane spinner wheels rolling across the ceramic expansion joints of the fifth-floor hallway.\n\nAt two-twenty-four, Shampa appeared at the entrance of the southern corridor.\n\nShe was wearing a charcoal wool blazer over an off-white silk blouse, black tailored trousers, and pointed leather flats. Her face was scrubbed of makeup, her eyes swollen and ringed with dark crimson rims, her hair tied in a messy, hurried bun held together by a single plastic pen.\n\nShe pulled her silver Rimowa suitcase behind her with one hand, clutching her leather handbag and a boarding pass stub in the other.\n\nAs she saw Rahul sitting on the bench, she stopped.\n\nShe looked at him. She looked at his rumpled beige cotton shirt, at his uncombed hair, at his bare forearms, at the blue Reliance Fresh plastic bag sitting between his shoes.\n\nFor three seconds, neither of them moved.\n\nThen Shampa let go of the suitcase handle. The heavy German luggage drifted six inches forward on its precision bearings before coming to a rest against the stainless steel handrail.\n\nShe walked toward him, her steps unsteady, her shoulders shaking under the wool jacket.\n\nRahul stood up.\n\nHe reached out his arms.\n\nShampa walked straight into his chest. She buried her face into the curve of his shoulder, her hands grabbing the fabric of his shirt with desperate, clawing intensity, her whole body shuddering with a violent, dry convulsion that shook them both.\n\nShe did not weep loudly.\n\nIn the middle-class families of Bengal, one does not scream in a hospital corridor; one absorbs the disaster into the linen of one's brother's shirt.\n\nRahul held her.\n\nHe felt the fragile, bird-like sharpness of her collarbones beneath the jacket. He remembered holding her hand forty years ago when their father had taken them to see the animals at Alipore Zoo, warning her not to put her fingers through the wire mesh of the deer enclosure.\n\n'Dada,' she whispered against his chest, her breath hot against his skin. 'Tell me he isn't going to die tonight. Please, Dada. Just tell me that.'\n\nRahul closed his eyes.\n\nHe could have lied. He could have used the standard, palliative phrases that relatives trade in corridors: *“He is strong, the doctors are hopeful, the next twenty-four hours are crucial.”*\n\nHe did not lie.\n\nThey were Debabrata Sen's children. Their father had spent fifty-two years checking the accounts of the postal department; he had despised false totals, rounded figures, and unearned comfort.\n\n'He is very sick, Shampa,' Rahul said softly into her hair. 'The bleeding is inside the basal ganglia. The volume is thirty-eight milliliters with significant midline shift. If he survives the night, he will never speak again. He will never walk again. He may never wake from the coma.'\n\nShampa pulled back.\n\nShe stared at her brother's face, her mouth slightly open, her eyes wide with a horrified, disbelieving fury.\n\n'How can you say that so calmly?' she whispered, her voice rising in the empty corridor. 'How can you just stand there and give me a medical prognosis like you're reading a balance sheet? He's your father!'\n\n'He is my father,' Rahul said quietly, his voice dropping into that terrifying, immovable stillness that was his father's true inheritance. 'And because he is my father, I will not insult him by pretending this is a common fever. He is dying, Shampa. And we have to help him die without making him suffer.'\n\nThe word *die* hung between them like an iron anvil dropped onto the vinyl floor.\n\nShampa stepped back. She touched her temple with trembling fingers, looking around at the closed doors, at the red digital clock, at the security guard sleeping by the exit.\n\n'I want to see him,' she said.\n\n'The ICU does not allow visitors after midnight,' Rahul said. 'Sister Roy told me she will let us in for five minutes at four-thirty before the morning blood draws.'\n\n'I am not waiting until four-thirty!' she said, her voice trembling with that familiar, imperious corporate authority. 'I am his daughter! I flew from Delhi in the middle of the night! I have the right to see my father!'\n\nShe turned and marched toward the double doors of frosted glass.\n\nShe reached for the stainless steel push-plate.\n\nBefore her palm could touch the metal, Rahul caught her wrist.\n\nHe did not squeeze hard, but his grip was unyielding.\n\n'Shampa. Stop.'\n\nShe struggled against his grip for two seconds, then looked into his eyes.\n\nShe saw the twenty-four hours of sleeplessness behind his spectacles. She saw the grey exhaustion, the quiet, profound devastation that had settled into the lines around his mouth.\n\nShe let her arm go limp.\n\nShe looked down at the floor between them.\n\nHer eyes fell on the blue Reliance Fresh bag.\n\n'What is that?' she asked, her voice cracking.\n\n'His slippers,' Rahul said softly. 'His umbrella. His spectacles. The keys to the flat.'\n\nShampa stared at the bag.\n\nThe rage went out of her like air escaping a punctured tire. She sank down onto the vinyl bench, covered her face with both hands, and sat in silence, her silver suitcase standing sentinel beside her in the corridor.",
      "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1519494026892-80bbd2d6fd0d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=85",
      "alt": "A person sitting alone on a long hospital corridor bench outside intensive care doors",
      "caption": "02:24 — The fifth floor. A silver suitcase, a charcoal blazer, and the word 'die' dropped like an iron anvil.",
      "imageSize": "portrait",
      "imageWidth": 1600,
      "imageHeight": 1067
