      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "IX. 04:40 — Bed 7 and the Petroleum Jelly",
      "heading": "IX. 04:40 — Bed 7 and the Petroleum Jelly",
      "body": "The air inside the Neuro-ICU was colder than the corridor, smelling of filtered oxygen, antiseptic chlorhexidine, and the sharp, ozone tang of high-voltage electronic displays.\n\nThere were eight beds arranged in a semi-circle around the central nursing monitoring desk. Six of the beds were occupied by silent, motionless figures beneath white blankets, their faces obscured by plastic breathing masks, nasal gastric tubes, and intravenous lines suspended from chrome ceiling tracks.\n\nBed 7 was in the far corner, beside an interior glass partition.\n\nSister Bandana Roy, the senior night charge nurse, was standing by the bedside infusion pumps when Rahul and Shampa walked through the sliding curtain.\n\nShe was a woman of forty-eight with iron-grey hair pulled back in a severe, immaculate bun, wearing a starched white nurse's uniform with three silver pens clipped to her breast pocket. She looked up as they entered. Her face had the calm, immovable authority of someone who had stood beside three thousand beds when the soul crossed the border between flesh and silence.\n\n'Leave your shoes outside the curtain,' Sister Roy whispered in Bengali, pointing to the plastic floor markers. 'Put on these yellow paper gowns.'\n\nThey tied the yellow gowns over their clothes.\n\nThey stepped to the side of the mechanical bed.\n\nDebabrata lay propped up at a thirty-degree angle.\n\nHe was wearing a white hospital gown printed with faded blue crosses. A transparent corrugated ventilator hose was taped to the right corner of his mouth with white surgical adhesive, running back to the stainless steel chassis of a ventilator that wheezed with rhythmic, unhurried precision:\n\n*Hiss... click... pause.*\n*Hiss... click... pause.*\n\nHis eyes were closed. His eyebrows, thick and grey, were relaxed, without the furrow of tension that had marked his forehead during his working years. His skin, usually the warm, weathered brown of a man who walked around the Dhakuria lakes every morning, had paled to the color of dry river clay.\n\nOn his right wrist was an arterial cannula connected to a pressure transducer, recording his blood pressure as a continuous, jagged red waveform on the overhead Philips monitor:\n\n*BP: 168/94. Pulse: 64. SpO2: 99%.*\n\nShampa reached out.\n\nHer trembling hand touched her father's left hand—the hand that was free of intravenous lines, resting limp on the white cotton sheet.\n\nHis fingers were cold. The fingernails, usually clipped short and clean with his old brass clipper, were slightly blue at the lunula.\n\nShampa picked up that cold hand and pressed it against her cheek.\n\n'Baba...' she whispered.\n\nShe did not say anything else. She stood with her cheek pressed against the rough, dry skin of his knuckles, her eyes closed, her breath warming the cold back of his hand.\n\nRahul stood at the head of the bed.\n\nHe looked at his father's face.\n\nWithout the dentures, his father's cheeks had sunk inward by an inch, giving his jaw a sharp, skeletal profile that made him look twenty years older—or perhaps twenty years younger, resembling the old black-and-white studio portrait of his grandfather that hung in the hallway of the Jadavpur flat.\n\nSister Roy stepped forward, carrying a small glass jar of white petroleum jelly and two sterile cotton-tipped applicators.\n\nShe touched Rahul's arm.\n\n'The ventilator pushes cold, dry air continuously through the mouth,' Sister Roy said in a low, gentle whisper. 'The mucosal tissue dries out very fast. It cracks and bleeds if you don't keep it moist. Take this.'\n\nShe handed Rahul the jar and the cotton swabs.\n\n'Apply it to his lips,' she said. 'Gently. Like this.'\n\nShe demonstrated with one swab, dipping it into the petroleum jelly and coating the right corner of Debabrata's lower lip with a smooth, glossy film.\n\nThen she handed the second swab to Rahul.\n\nRahul took the wooden stick.\n\nHis hand was shaking so hard that the cotton tip tapped against the chrome bed rail with a faint, dry *tink*.\n\nHe leaned over the bed.\n\nHe looked at his father's mouth—the mouth that had told him stories from the *Mahabharata* when he had the chickenpox in 1986; the mouth that had read the poems of Rabindranath Tagore at the Sunday tea table; the mouth that had scolded him when he had scratched his bicycle against the municipal lamppost.\n\nHe dipped the swab into the jelly.\n\nHe touched the cotton to his father's upper lip.\n\nThe skin was rough, parched, cracked into tiny, microscopic fissures like dry mud in a summer paddy field.\n\nRahul smoothed the jelly across the lip.\n\nHe moved with agonizing, trembling gentleness, coating the dry skin with the translucent, protective grease, making sure not to disturb the white surgical tape that held the endotracheal tube in place.\n\nAs the cotton swab touched the left corner of his mouth, something happened.\n\nDebabrata's left eyelid twitched.\n\nIt was not a conscious movement; it was a brainstem reflex, a tiny electrical discharge from a dying cranial nerve.\n\nBut from the inner corner of that closed left eye, a single, clear tear emerged.\n\nThe drop welled up against the grey eyelashes, spilled over the lower lid, and ran slowly down the pale, hollow cheek, leaving a wet, shining track through the stubble before coming to rest against the edge of the cotton pillowcase.\n\nShampa gasped.\n\nShe looked up at Rahul, her eyes wide with terror and wonder.\n\n'Dada!' she whispered. 'Did you see that? He can hear us! Baba can hear us!'\n\nRahul did not answer.\n\nHe did not look at the monitor. He did not consult his statistics.\n\nHe reached out his bare hand, touched his father's temple where the grey hair was soft as thistledown, and bent down until his mouth was two inches from his father's ear.\n\n'Baba,' Rahul whispered into the cold ear.\n\nHis voice was steady, clear, filled with forty-four years of gratitude that had never found words in the busy rooms of life.\n\n'Baba, it is Rahul. Shampa is here. We are both here. The flat in Jadavpur is safe. The doors are locked. The keys are in my pocket. You took care of us for forty years, Baba. You never let our socks get wet in the flood.'\n\nHe touched the tear on his father's cheek with the tip of his finger.\n\n'You can rest now, Baba. The accounts are balanced. You don't have to keep the watch wound anymore.'\n\nDebabrata did not move.\n\nThe ventilator hissed with its rhythmic, indifferent cadence: *three seconds in, two seconds out*.\n\nBeside the bed, Shampa knelt down on the linoleum floor, buried her face in the white sheet beside her father's hip, and stayed there, motionless, breathing in the smell of the father who had loved her before the world had ever given her a name.",
