      "heading": "X. 05:30 — The Grey Dawn over the Lake",
      "body": "At five-thirty, Sister Roy came to the curtain and touched Rahul's shoulder.\n\n'The morning blood gas analysis is due,' she said softly. 'You must wait in the corridor now. The doctors will begin their rounds at seven.'\n\nThey untied the yellow paper gowns and dropped them into the stainless steel pedal bin by the door.\n\nThey stepped back through the frosted glass doors into the corridor.\n\nThe fifth floor had begun its slow, nocturnal surrender to the morning.\n\nAt the eastern end of the long hallway was a massive panoramic window of reinforced acoustic glass that looked out over the Dhakuria lakes and the rooftops of south Kolkata.\n\nRahul and Shampa walked toward that window, side by side, their footsteps silent on the polished vinyl.\n\nOutside, the darkness was dissolving.\n\nThe night sky had turned from coal-black to a soft, watery, translucent slate-grey—the color of river pearls, the color of wet Deccan slate, the color of dawn over Bengal.\n\nBelow the window, through the rising morning mist, the city was beginning to wake.\n\nThe streetlights along the Southern Avenue boulevard flickered and died in unison as the municipal timer clicked off. Along the perimeter railing of the Rabindra Sarobar, the first morning walkers had appeared: elderly men in woollen mufflers and sports shoes, swinging their arms in brisk, disciplined circles; tea vendors in blue lungis setting up their kerosene stoves under blue tarpaulin awnings; the first yellow Ambassador taxi rattling over the railway bridge, its round chrome headlights cutting two pale cones through the fog.\n\nRahul pulled his phone from his pocket.\n\nThe screen was black. The battery had reached zero percent; the device was dead, cold, inert—a useless slab of glass and aluminium.\n\nHe felt an extraordinary, sudden lightness in his chest.\n\nNo more emails. No more WhatsApp groups. No more administrative notifications from the university.\n\nThere was only the grey sky, the waking lake, and his sister standing beside him.\n\nShampa leaned her head against the glass.\n\nThe cold of the exterior pane fogged under her breath, creating a tiny, circular mist that expanded and contracted with her breathing: *in, out, in, out*.\n\n'What do we do now, Dada?' she asked softly.\n\nShe did not ask it like a corporate director seeking an action item. She asked it like a ten-year-old girl standing on the platform at Howrah station, holding her brother's hand while the crowds moved around them.\n\nRahul looked down at the street.\n\nHe reached down to the floor and picked up the blue Reliance Fresh plastic bag.\n\nHe held it by its handles.\n\nInside, the four keys clinked together with a soft, clean, metallic chime—the music of home, the music of thirty years of doors being opened and locked in the quiet afternoons of Jadavpur.\n\n'We wait,' Rahul said.\n\nHis voice was calm, clear, filled with the vast, immovable peace that comes when the worst thing that can happen in human life has already arrived, has been looked in the face, and has been found to contain no malice—only the ancient, necessary gravity of the earth.\n\n'We wait for Dr. Roy,' Rahul continued, putting his arm around his sister's shoulders. 'And when the rounds are finished, we will go down to the street. We will drink two glasses of hot tea from the vendor by the gate. We will buy a loaf of fresh bread from the bakery. And then we will go back to Jadavpur, unlock the door, open the windows, and let the morning air into his room.'\n\nShampa looked at him.\n\nShe nodded slowly. She reached out and took one of the plastic handles of the blue bag, holding it together with him.\n\nTwo children of an accounts superintendent, standing on the fifth floor of a hospital in the fading night, holding their father's slippers and spectacles between them, watching the first gold light of the morning sun strike the water of the lake; and in the vast, unbreakable silence of their shared grief, they turned their faces toward the dawn and waited.",
